WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.360
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.020
With a new Rick and Morty season supposedly
just around the corner we thought that it

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.900
would be a great time to revisit one of our
favorite episodes: "Get Schwifty."

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.060
"Get Schwifty" was a jam.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:21.320
One of the show's essential features is that
it trivializes everything people hold dear,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.180
and for billions of people, nothing is more
sacred than religion.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.420
While religion is, rather overtly, a regular
victim of Rick's contempt,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.000
there's something to be said about the complexity of the Schwifty episode.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.160
While Rick and Morty often reduces religion
to superstition,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.380
"There is no god Summer. Gotta rip that bandaid off now, you'll thank me later."

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.660
"Get Schwifty" complicates this outlook, exploring
the way we rationalize such thinking.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.380
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy of "Get Schwifty".

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.100
When a giant floating head shows up and insists
to humanity,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.040
"SHOW ME WHAT YOU GOT! I WANT TO SEE WHAT YOU GOT!"

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:03.140
the Earth is plagued with earthquakes, sink holes, fires, and massive flooding.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.980
The show uses this opportunity to quickly
roast the opportunistic nature of organized religion.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:13.720
Giant heads are destroying the world, but the church needs more faith, more money, and a new church organ.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:20.180
"If sanity has been restored to this assembly, I'd like to talk about the cost of repairing our new organ."

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:21.820
But it doesn't dwell there.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:27.520
The episode follows two groups. There are those that know the Cromulons are actually there to film a reality show,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.540
"This head won't go away until Earth shows them it's got, a hit song!"

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.300
and those that have no clue what's going on.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.360
"Is it God? If it's God, do we get out of school?"

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.040
Headism is a cult that pops up because of
a cosmic problem in point of view.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.220
There's no peak behind the curtain.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.660
People don't know what the hell a Cromulon
is or what "show me what you've got" refers to,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.800
"SHOW ME WHAT YOU GOT!"

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.540
so naturally, they form a cult.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.820
The episode could be read as just a scathing
indictment of religion, about the arbitrary

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.100
nature of faith and its ceremonies.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.700
Like on "Ascension Day" the Headists send "unwantables"to the gods,

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.740
which bears a striking resemblance to the crucifixion of Christ.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.920
Instead, what is presented is something much
more complex.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.020
The episode takes aim at the reasoning behind belief structures themselves, not just religious practices,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.660
it exposes the fallacies behind
all kinds of shit people believe.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.300
"Get Schwifty" makes fun of two common logical fallacies,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.360
both of which conflate correlation with causation.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.900
The Headist religion is started because Principal Vagina prays to the Head at the same time

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.920
that the cataclysmic events begin to subside:

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.260
"Whaddaya know? He was right!"

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.400
That's the Cum Hoc Ergo Propter Hoc fallacy:
the mistaken belief that one thing occurring

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.720
at the same time as another unrelated thing
has anything to do with each other.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.700
The reality is the Heads stop because they are pleased with Rick's impromptu performance of "Get Schwifty."

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.760
A song about dropping your panties, and a
deuce, on the floor.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:57.000
"Take off your pants and your panties. Sh*t on the floor. Time to get schwifty in here!"

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:03.340
"I LIKE WHAT YOU GOT. GOOD JOB!"

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.040
The person to create the first list of logical
fallacies was Aristotle.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.409
Tired of arguing with dumb people, he created a giant list of arguments that sounded great

00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:13.709
but are actually pure ass-clownery.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:16.340
The episode plays with all sorts of these
fallacies.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.180
The President thinks that The Dream, Billy Corgan, Randy Newman, and Pharrell are all dead because:

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.140
"They're all dead."

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.740
"What? How is that possible? Do people just die when I name them?"

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.000
That's the Post Hoc Ergo Proctor Hoc fallacy: the idea that because something

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.019
comes prior to something else it is naturally the cause.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:37.680
Principal Vagina tries to ascend Beth and Jerry, and everybody assumes that this angers the heads.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.000
"Looks like the heads are getting angry!"

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.500
*Heads start booing*

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.180
"The heads are displeased!"

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.560
That's the Cum Hoc Ergo Propter Hoc fallacy again.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.220
In reality the heads are actually pissed off because
Rick's performance without Morty is terrible.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:52.820
"Ugh, tough crowd."

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.500
That and a nuclear strike.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.920
"BOO, NOT COOL. DISQUALIFIED!"

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.020
So the Headists try to ascend Principal Vagina to ease the heads anger

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.090
but truth is, the heads don't give a rat's ass about what they're up to.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.190
In each instance, the characters with a limited point of view,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.880
the people who don't know that all of this is
just a giant reality TV show,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.900
believe that their mundane ceremonies actually influence the Heads.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.580
Beth and Jerry actually point out this logical fallacy a couple times.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:26.820
"It's possible that we may have been correlating some things that weren't actually related at all."

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.080
"I'm just saying, we don't know there's a cause-effect relationship—"

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.960
While ruthlessly satirizing the magical thinking of the faithful, the episode also makes a greater point.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.760
People don't just fall into silly beliefs out of pure irrationality —

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:44.400
rather, they over rationalize their thinking to the point
of absurdity.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.600
Some cognitive psychologists argue that pseudo-scientific belief systems are far from fragile.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.100
Doomsday cults, psychics, and conspiracy theorists
are all so resilient in their beliefs because

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.940
they are sustained by the structure of the
human brain.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.520
The belief systems persist and spread via
appeals to rationality,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.400
not through tricking people or the stupidity of the masses.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.980
The human brain is hard-wired to search for
logical conclusions and easy explanations.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.860
"How is praying going to help?"

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.980
"Ma'am, a giant head in the sky is controlling the weather. Did you wanna play checkers?"

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.520
"Let's be rational! I'll see you at God's house!"

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.660
People tend to believe what they want to believe — what
lines up with their world view.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:25.040
People like consuming and sharing news that
aligns with their political beliefs or creating

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.410
elaborate justifications for supporting the
sportsball team they were raised to root for..

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:34.080
Its called confirmation bias: the propensity
to seek out and interpret information that

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.620
proves your own beliefs.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:39.911
Confirmation bias explains why people are
willing to overcome glaring flaws in their belief

00:05:39.911 --> 00:05:42.271
system for the sake of 
personal benefit.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:48.100
"Summer is doing really well here. She's not getting pregnant, or doing drugs, or missing curfew..."

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.920
"Please help me. you can reach me if you try! PLEASE HELP ME!"

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.780
"That's not our business as long as Summer is thriving."

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:57.380
All of these fallacies and biases aren't unique
to religion though.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:01.240
While Rick and Morty has a great time
shitting on religion, the reality is that

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:05.880
post hoc rationalization is an inevitability
of our cause and effect thought process.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:12.020
Even the audience is fooled by the brain's tendency to accept logical fallacies as seen in Rick's snake and wristwatch trick.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:19.080
"Stay back! This watch turns people into snakes!"

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.000
"Stand down! Everybody, stand down!"

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:32.040
It Plays on the brains desire to attribute
event A as the cause of event B. When in reality:

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.780
"Why didn't he turn into a snake?"

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:40.720
"Trade secret Mr. President. Particle beam in the wristwatch, snake holster on the leg."

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.640
Sure, Rick and Morty delivers the tasty irony
that the cosmic events that help build a religion

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:50.290
can be woefully inaccurate, that the reality
of belief structures can be built on the most

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:53.850
inane of all things — a f**king reality TV
show.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.860
But they take things a step further.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.820
The ability to make predictions and understand the complexity of existence as a whole is taken to task.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:05.120
And that's what separates Rick and Morty from
contemporary satire.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:06.460
Nothing is safe.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:07.900
Not even belief.

